{
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "mentions_hardware_device",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.85,
      "source_groundedness": 0.6,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "A binary flag for hardware-device mentions is a genuine signal axis directly relevant to identifying hardware problems in descriptions. Grounding is mostly sound on the software rows (correctly 0), but the MySQL row is flagged 1 as a 'hardware device' when 'MySQL server' is a software/service, not physical hardware -- an unwarranted reading that lowers groundedness."
    },
    {
      "name": "mentions_physical_symptom",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.85,
      "source_groundedness": 0.9,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Physical-symptom mentions (e.g., won't power on, overheating, noise) are a strong distinct signal for hardware problems and add a real axis. Sampled values are all 0 and consistent with the software/connection descriptions shown, which contain no physical symptoms."
    },
    {
      "name": "is_hardware_category",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.5,
      "source_groundedness": 0.9,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Derivable from the existing 'category' column, so it is largely redundant with an original field rather than extracted from the description; still, aligning category to a hardware flag has some comparative value. Sampled values are consistent (0 for software/connection rows)."
    },
    {
      "name": "likely_hardware_problem",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.75,
      "source_groundedness": 0.6,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "A composite likelihood label is useful as a target/grouping variable for the query. However it appears to inherit the mislabeling: the MySQL connection row is marked likely_hardware_problem=1 despite being a database/connectivity (software) issue, an inference the source text does not support."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.75,
  "source_groundedness": 0.72,
  "rationale": "The added columns give genuine, query-relevant signal axes (device mentions, physical symptoms, and a composite hardware label) for analyzing what description features indicate hardware problems; is_hardware_category is weaker as it largely restates the existing category field. Groundedness is generally sound for the software rows, but the MySQL-server row is treated as a hardware device / likely hardware problem, which misreads a software/connectivity issue as physical hardware, introducing observable hallucination."
}

